  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Mechanistic path : CFTR → dysbiosis → inflammation**\n\n**1. CFTR loss drives intestinal dysbiosis**  \n- **Animal – intestinal‑specific Cftr knockout**: iCftr‑KO mice (both PEG‑laxative and liquid‑diet regimens) develop a marked shift in fecal microbiota (↑Proteobacteria, Enterobacteriaceae, Clostridia; ↓α‑diversity in liquid diet) compared with WT littermates, demonstrating that loss of CFTR in the epitheli is sufficient to remodel the gut community【PMID:37546931】 (animal).  \n- **Animal – whole‑body CFTR‑KO vs gut‑corrected CFTR‑KO**: Global CFTR‑KO mice show enrichment of Enterobacteriaceae/Enterococcus and increased gut permeability; restoring CFTR only in the intestine (CFTR‑KO‑GC) normalizes the microbiota and eliminates dysbiosis, proving that defective intestinal CFTR sustains the pathogenic microbiota【PMID:38554847】 (animal).  \n- **Human systematic review**: Across 38 studies, people with CF consistently exhibit reduced microbial diversity and a taxonomic profile (↑pro‑inflammatory taxa, ↓anti‑inflammatory taxa) that is attributed to CFTR‑related changes in the gut milieu (e.g., altered ion/HCO₃⁻ secretion, pancreatic insufficiency)【PMID:36600119】 (human).  \n\n**2. Dysbiosis contributes to intestinal and systemic inflammation**  \n- **Animal – iCftr‑KO inflammation**: The same iCftr‑KO mice display elevated fecal calprotectin (a marker of mucosal inflammation) that co‑varies with the dysbiotic community (e.g., Proteobacteria expansion)【PMID:37546931】 (animal).  \n- **Animal – CFTR‑KO liver inflammation**: In CFTR‑KO mice, dysbiosis (Enterobacteriaceae enrichment) is linked to increased intestinal permeability, mucosal inflammation, and downstream peri‑portal liver inflammation; non‑absorbable antibiotics that blunt dysbiosis also reduce liver inflammation, indicating a causal role of the dysbiotic microbiota【PMID:38554847】 (animal).  \n- **Human – CFTR modulator effect**: In 16 CF patients treated with ivacaftor, reduced stool calprotectin coincides with an increase in Akkermansia and a decrease in Enterobacteriaceae; the correlation between microbial shifts and lower inflammation supports a mechanistic link【PMID:30546102】 (human).  \n- **Animal – Bacteroides‑derived propionate**: Supplementing Bacteroides (a SCFA‑producing genus depleted in CF) raises fecal propionate and lowers systemic pro‑inflammatory cytokines; propionate directly dampens IL‑1β‑driven inflammation in CFTR‑deficient Caco‑2 cells, illustrating how loss of specific microbes can drive inflammation【PMID:38179971】 (animal + in vitro).  \n\n**3. Testable"
  },
